3. Raw TypeScript checking before Astro sync reported 19 missing generated content-type errors in unchanged feed/sitemap sources. Normal `astro sync` generated the local type artifacts; the same strict TypeScript command then passed. No unrelated fixes were made.
4. The first full unit run completed 4,999 passing tests, 12 failing tests and 13 skipped tests, with two additional suite-load failures. Nine failures came from absent normal-build dist pages; one from the generated manifest, one from the new source fingerprint needing root's capture carrier, and one from a Registry test's old comment anchor. The Registry anchor was updated without changing its semantic assertions. The two suite-load failures were the already diagnosed Vite external-symlink path problem for Astronomy Engine. The exact same eight package files were copied into the isolated checkout's own node_modules; all byte hashes match, no package installation/version change occurred, and both suites passed in the final 227-test affected run. The original full result is retained and is **not** presented as a green full-suite run.
5. The first optional C014 native comparison was invoked with 9d180c9, even though that driver's baseline control deliberately requires pre-C014 skipped-date success. Eight failures reflected that wrong baseline. Four additional old-Moon failures arose because its retired keys were being resolved against the candidate catalogs. The original 23/35 report remains. The corrected run uses immutable `3845a04b` (full hash in before-c014-identity.json) with all eight matching component/catalog sources and their executed hashes; all 35 controls pass. No refusal or numerical expectation was weakened. The driver now substitutes the baseline's own catalogs when its optional historical baseline is requested.

The new 34-group native caption/image driver passed on its first execution. Its initially copied driver bytes are retained and match the final frozen driver. The source was not edited during that run. All later source-freeze checks are explicit.

